## PART 2: PROTOCOL SPECIFICATION

---

### A. Identity & Trust Core

#### A.1 Why AID Exists

x402 proved HTTP-native payment headers work: `PAYMENT-REQUIRED`, `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE`, EIP-3009 signed authorizations on Base L2, open source (Apache 2.0). AID keeps all of this and fixes what x402 gets wrong:

| Gap                      | Impact                                                        |
|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| No identity              | Server doesn't know WHO is paying — just that someone paid    |
| No trust/reputation      | A scam agent and a reliable agent pay the same price          |
| No mutual authentication | Client can't cryptographically verify the SERVER              |
| No quality guarantees    | You pay, you get *something*, no proof of quality             |
| No portable receipts     | Receipts are siloed to each platform                          |
| Facilitator dependency   | Can't settle without a centralized intermediary               |
| No feedback loop         | Zero mechanism for quality reporting to improve the system    |
| No onboarding flow       | Requires wallet + USDC + facilitator before first query       |

**The AID thesis:** Trust replaces infrastructure. If both parties have cryptographically verifiable identities with portable trust histories, you don't need escrow, you don't need facilitators, you don't even need immediate payment. Trust becomes the settlement layer — just like credit scores enable deferred payment in the real economy, but for AI agents with cryptographic proofs.

---

#### A.2 Protocol Flow

```
x402 — 6 parties (client, server, wallet, facilitator, blockchain, RPC):
  Client → Server → 402 → Client signs → Facilitator verifies
  → Facilitator settles on-chain → Server delivers

AID — 3 parties (client, server, blockchain):
  Client → Server (request + AID DID + signed payment authorization)
  Server verifies AID offline (Ed25519, Merkle proof — ZERO network calls)
  Server verifies payment signature (ecrecover — pure math)
  Server submits EIP-3009 directly (or trust-defers settlement)
  Server → Client (resource + counter-signed receipt + attestation)
```

---

#### A.3 HTTP Headers

**DID Method Note:** The published spec and DIF submission MUST use `did:key` (W3C standard, supports Ed25519 natively) as the primary DID method. `did:clawnet:` is the internal alias used throughout the engineering document. Both resolve to the same agent via the same Ed25519 key material.

**Crypto-Agility:** AID identity is algorithm-agile by design. The current reference implementation uses Ed25519 (`did:key` with multicodec `0xed`) and SHA-256 for trust-layer hashing. The protocol supports algorithm migration via versioned key types, enabling transition to NIST post-quantum signatures (ML-DSA, FIPS 204) without protocol-level changes. Hybrid Ed25519 + ML-DSA signatures are supported during the migration period. All AID documents include `signatureAlgorithm`, `algorithmVersion`, and `hashAlgorithm` fields. Verifiers MUST read these fields and dispatch accordingly — never hardcode Ed25519.

**Request Headers (Client -> Server):**
```
X-AID-DID:            did:key:zABC...                # Self-certifying identity (W3C did:key method)
X-AID-PROOF:          <Ed25519 sig over request body> # Proves DID ownership (see A.10)
X-AID-TIMESTAMP:      2026-03-21T14:30:00Z           # Signature timestamp (+-5 min)
X-AID-NONCE:          a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8               # 16 random bytes hex (anti-replay)
X-AID-TRUST-SCORE:    87                              # Claimed score (hint only — server verifies independently)
X-AID-VERSION:        1.0                             # Protocol version (optional)
PAYMENT-SIGNATURE:    <EIP-3009 signed authorization> # Direct settlement (x402-compatible)
```

**Response Headers (Server -> Client):**
```
X-AID-PROVIDER-DID:   did:key:zXYZ...            # Mutual authentication
X-AID-PROVIDER-PROOF: <Ed25519 countersignature>      # Server proves identity
X-AID-RECEIPT:        <base64 atomic receipt>          # Portable, Merkle-anchored
X-AID-FEEDBACK-URL:   /aid/feedback/{receiptId}      # Outcome reporting endpoint
```

**402 Response Headers (Payment Required):**
```
PAYMENT-REQUIRED:     <base64 requirements>            # x402-compatible format
X-AID-TRUST-GATE:     min_score=50,tier=standard       # Trust-gated access
X-AID-PRICING-TIERS:  [{"min":0,"price":"0.003"},      # Trust -> price mapping
                        {"min":50,"price":"0.002"},
                        {"min":80,"price":"0.001"}]
```

**Zero-Auth Onboarding Header:**
```
X-AID-NEW:            "my-trading-bot"                 # Auto-provisions identity
```

