#### A.5.1 Input Integrity Model (Hard vs Soft Inputs)

> **Added March 24, 2026. Addresses external feedback on fabrication resistance.**

AID's scoring formula explicitly separates inputs by fabrication cost. Inputs that require real economic commitment ("hard") dominate scoring; inputs that a provider can self-report ("soft") are heavily discounted. This ensures Sybil attackers must commit real resources to reach higher tiers.

**Input classification:**

| Input Type | Classification | Weight | Why |
|------------|---------------|--------|-----|
| Agreed DSIRs (both parties sign, both classify outcome) | **Hard** | 1.0 | Requires real counterparty + real transaction + real payment. Cannot be fabricated without economic cost. |
| On-chain settlement evidence (txHash, block confirmation) | **Hard** | 0.7 | Publicly verifiable, immutable. Costs real gas + real USDC. |
| Dual-party commitment logs (Merkle-anchored, cross-referenced) | **Hard** | 0.7 | Both parties independently commit — selective omission is detectable. |
| Attestations with receipt reference | **Medium** | 0.5 | Server-generated but references verifiable receipt. Harder to fabricate than pure self-report. |
| Oracle-provided scores (ERC-8004, Cred, external) | **Medium** | 0.3 | External source, but oracle itself could be compromised. Capped at `building` tier (score 39). |
| Disputed DSIRs (parties disagree on outcome) | **Soft** | 0.3 | Disagreement reduces confidence. Dispute rate feeds superlinear integrity penalty. |
| Self-declared inputs (manifests, capability claims) | **Soft** | 0.1 | Provider-controlled. Manifest adherence is verified post-hoc but initial declaration is free. |

**How hard inputs dominate tier placement:**

```
SYBIL ATTACK (soft inputs only):
  Self-declared manifests (0.1) + self-reported success (0.1) = max effective weight 0.2
  → Score capped around 20 (building tier, base price, no discount)
  → Cannot reach "caution" (40+) without hard inputs

LEGITIMATE AGENT (hard inputs available):
  Agreed DSIRs (1.0) + on-chain settlement (0.7) + commitment logs (0.7)
  → Full scoring weight, tier placement based on actual performance
  → "proceed" (90+) requires 6mo of hard-input history + $50 revenue

THE KEY INSIGHT:
  Determinism helps verification (anyone can reproduce the score).
  Input integrity helps resistance (soft inputs can't reach high tiers).
  AID has both.
```

**Relationship to existing defenses:** The hard/soft classification works WITH counterpartyDiversity (v1.1), EigenTrust (10/10 Day 3), and dual-party commitment logs (10/10 Day 1) — not instead of them. counterpartyDiversity ensures hard inputs come from diverse sources. EigenTrust suppresses Sybil clusters even if they generate hard-looking transactions. Dual-party logs ensure hard inputs aren't selectively omitted.

