#### D.7 RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures Alignment

RFC 9421 (HTTP Message Signatures) is the IETF standard for signing HTTP messages. AID's custom signing format is cryptographically equivalent but uses non-standard headers (`X-AID-PROOF` instead of `Signature`/`Signature-Input`).

**Migration path (Phase 1):**
```
CURRENT (custom headers):
  X-AID-PROOF: <base64url Ed25519 signature>
  X-AID-TIMESTAMP: 2026-03-21T14:30:00Z
  X-AID-NONCE: a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8

RFC 9421 ALIGNED:
  Signature-Input: aid=("@method" "@path" "x-aid-did" "x-aid-nonce"
                        "content-digest");created=1711029000;keyid="did:key:zABC..."
                        ;alg="ed25519";nonce="a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8"
  Signature: aid=:base64-encoded-ed25519-signature:
  Content-Digest: sha-256=:base64-encoded-hash:
  X-AID-DID: did:key:zABC...
  X-AID-NONCE: a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8

BENEFITS:
  - Standard Signature-Input/Signature headers parseable by any RFC 9421 library
  - Content-Digest replaces custom body hashing
  - keyid="did:key:..." links signature to DID identity
  - alg="ed25519" is explicit (no algorithm confusion)
  - created= replaces X-AID-TIMESTAMP
  - Existing HTTP signature verification libraries work out of the box
```

The Ed25519 signature and security properties are IDENTICAL. Only the header format changes. Implementations should accept BOTH formats during transition (custom headers for backwards compat, RFC 9421 as the canonical format going forward).

